# Cleaning in a script, not in the spreadsheet {#sec-eda-data-quality}













In @sec-eda-first-look, one table was enough to catch three problems in `cell_data`: a column name with a typo (`Tisue_Area`), another with the wrong receptor spelled out (`Pdgfbr_MeanInt`), and a measurement written down twice in two different units. You now know what is wrong. The question that keeps me up at night on a project like this is a different one:

::: {style="text-align: center"}
***How do I fix it so that someone else, or me in six months, can repeat exactly what I did?***
:::

The tempting answer is to open the spreadsheet, correct the names, delete the extra column, save. It takes thirty seconds. I have done it, and I have also stared at a corrected file half a year later unable to say which cells I had touched or why. This chapter is about the alternative: auditing and repairing the data in code that sits next to the analysis, so that the repair is itself part of the record.

## Why the fix belongs in code

Think about how you treat a gel or a microscope image. You never "improve" the original file. You keep the raw acquisition untouched, you apply a documented adjustment (a crop, a brightness change applied to the whole image), and you can say exactly what was done. Editing a gel image by hand, with no record, is the textbook example of what a reviewer should not accept. A hand-corrected spreadsheet is the same act in a different medium: the raw measurement is overwritten, and the only evidence of what changed lives in your memory (@fig-quality-glass).

![The raw file is the original gel image: it stays under glass, byte for byte as it arrived. Every repair happens on a copy, in a script.](images/eda-data-quality_raw-under-glass.png){#fig-quality-glass fig-align="center" width="90%" fig-alt="Watercolor sketch of a pristine sheet of gridded paper resting on a dark cushion inside a glass display case on a wooden pedestal. At a worktable beside it, a smiling researcher in a lab coat and green sweater, chin on one hand, makes pencil marks on a copy of the same sheet, with an eraser, a pencil cup and an open notebook nearby."}

A script is the lab notebook entry for the data. It says *this column was renamed, this one was dropped, and here is the check that shows the two were the same measurement*. Anyone can rerun it on the raw file and obtain the identical result, which is the practical meaning of traceability that @sec-appendix-open-science argues for. And the spreadsheet itself has hazards beyond forgetfulness: it will happily reinterpret a gene name as a date, or round a value on save, without a word.

::: callout-important
### The rule for the rest of this book

**I never edit the raw CSV, and I do not rename its columns for the other chapters.** Every later chapter reads `project/AnalysisData/Cells_Gfap-Pdgfrb.csv` and uses the original names (`Tissue_Area`, `Gfap_IntDen`, and so on). All the cleaning in this chapter happens in a local object called `cell_clean`, which lives and dies inside this file.

I made that choice deliberately. The models in the linear-model chapters and beyond are written against the original names and cached to disk, and a book that renames columns halfway through forces you to re-learn its vocabulary at exactly the moment you are trying to learn something harder. (Where a chapter needs the area, it takes `Tissue_Area` straight from the raw file, since the unit duplicate is the one repair every later chapter agrees on.) It also keeps the raw file the single source of truth: if you open it in a text editor, it is byte for byte what the lab handed over. The cost is that the typos survive in the later chapters; the gain is that nothing you read after this page silently depends on a cleaning step you might have skipped.
:::

## Tidying names and finding duplicates with `janitor`

Base R can do most of what follows, and I have written the `gsub()` calls to prove it. I reach for `janitor` [@janitor] anyway, for the same reason I reach for a labeled multichannel pipette instead of a handwritten tip box: the common operations have names that say what they do, so the script reads like a protocol. The first one is `clean_names()`, which converts every column to consistent `snake_case`. The reason to want it is not aesthetics. Mixed conventions such as `Centroid.Y`, `Gfap_sdInt` and `PdgfrbHigh_Intensity` are a steady source of "object not found" errors, because you have to remember which style each column happens to use. @tbl-quality-names shows what it does to the names in `cell_data`.






::: {#tbl-quality-names .cell tbl-cap='What `clean_names()` does to the names in `cell_data`. Only the rows where the name changes are shown. Note that it normalizes the style but does not repair the typos: `tisue_area` and `pdgfbr_mean_int` are still wrong.'}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(janitor)

# Pair every original name with its cleaned version, keeping only the ones that change
names_map <- tibble(
  original = names(cell_data),
  cleaned = names(clean_names(cell_data))
) |>
  filter(original != cleaned)

# Show the pairs as a table
names_map |>
  gt() |>
  cols_label(original = "Original name", cleaned = "After clean_names()")
```

