No duplicated `MouseID`, which is what we hoped for (each row is one animal), and no fully repeated rows either (the `0` at the end). I mention it because pasting the same export twice is one of the more common ways a duplicated row sneaks in, and it costs one line to rule out.

One more `janitor` tool deserves a mention for this dataset specifically. `remove_constant()` drops columns that have a single value, since a variable that never varies cannot tell a model anything. In the full table nothing is constant. But later chapters filter `cell_data` to subsets, and then it becomes relevant: once you keep only the MCAO animals, `Condition` has one level left.






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Keep only MCAO animals, drop columns with a single value, count what is left
cell_data |>
  filter(Condition == "MCAO") |>
  remove_constant(quiet = FALSE) |>
  ncol()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] 19
```


:::
:::






The message says `Condition` was removed, leaving 19 columns. It is a gentle reminder that `Condition` carries information only because SHAM animals exist at all, and that it stops being a usable predictor the moment you model within MCAO. That is the same design limit we will meet when we fit models on the DPI 3, 7 and 14 subset in @sec-linear-regression-multiple. A last `janitor` function, `compare_df_cols()`, lines up the column names and types of several data frames at once. It earns its keep when a collaborator sends a second batch of animals and you need to know whether it can be stacked under the first; we have a single file here, so I will leave it as a pointer.

## The two typos and the duplicated column

Now the real cases. I will take the duplicated column first, because it is the one where I can show you proof rather than a suspicion.

### `Tissue_Area` and `Tisue_Area`

If `Tisue_Area` is really the same measurement in µm², then multiplying `Tissue_Area` (mm²) by 10^6^ should reproduce it. A statement like "the histograms look identical" is persuasive, but a numerical check is the thing you can put in a script and rerun. I use `all.equal()` rather than `==` because floating-point numbers that are equal on paper rarely are bit for bit, and I also print the largest absolute gap so you can judge it yourself.






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Do mm2 x 10^6 and the um2 column agree, allowing for floating-point error?
all.equal(cell_data$Tissue_Area * 1e6, cell_data$Tisue_Area)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] TRUE
```


:::

```{.r .cell-code}
# The largest absolute gap between the two, so you can judge it yourself
max(abs(cell_data$Tissue_Area * 1e6 - cell_data$Tisue_Area))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] 9.313226e-10
```


:::
:::






The two columns agree to better than a billionth of a µm², far below any rounding that matters. They are one measurement, and I can drop either without losing information. Which one to keep is then a question of convenience and clarity, and I keep **`Tissue_Area`**, in mm², for three reasons. The first is the one a bench scientist feels before any statistician does: a mm² is an area you can picture (a brain section is a few of them), while a figure like 12,500,000 µm² means nothing to the eye. The second is that small numbers are easier to work with than large ones. A median near 12 is friendly to the priors we will write and to the sampler, while a predictor in the tens of millions invites exactly the scaling trouble that `brms` priors and Stan's adaptation have to work around. The third is consistency: `Tissue_Area` is the column I analyze everywhere in the book. The offset in @sec-count-regression, where the area is the exposure over which cells were counted, uses it, and so do the regression chapters, so `cell_clean` and `cell_data` agree on the one name that matters most.

::: callout-note
### Where the second column came from

This one I can answer without guessing, because I made the file. When I measured the area in FIJI, the software gave it to me in µm², and I converted it to mm² myself because that is the unit this field can reason about. So `Tisue_Area` is the raw output of the image-analysis software (typo in the name included), and `Tissue_Area` is the derived, human-readable version. The numerical check above agrees with that account, but it could never have told us the direction of the conversion on its own; only the provenance does. That is why I write it down in a comment next to the cleaning code instead of leaving it in my head. If you inherit a file like this from someone else, the places to look are the acquisition notes and the export macro or measurement table (pixel size and scale calibration in FIJI, for example), and the person who exported it.
:::

### `Pdgfbr_MeanInt`

Here the evidence is thinner, so the reasoning has to be more careful. Every neighbor of this column spells the receptor `Pdgfrb` (`Pdgfrb_Area`, `Pdgfrb_sdInt`, `Pdgfrb_IntDen`), and the GFAP block has a complete `Gfap_Area`, `Gfap_MeanInt`, `Gfap_sdInt`, `Gfap_IntDen` set. The PDGFR-β block has a gap exactly where `Pdgfrb_MeanInt` would sit. That is a strong hint that `Pdgfbr_MeanInt` is a transposition of letters in that column's name, not a different quantity (@fig-quality-pegboard).

![Every set has its place. When one outline sits empty and its lookalike hangs crooked elsewhere, the likeliest story is a mislabeled tool, not a new one.](images/eda-data-quality_pegboard-missing-tool.png){#fig-quality-pegboard fig-align="center" width="90%" fig-alt="Watercolor sketch of a pegboard with two rows of tools. The top row has a hammer, a wrench, a screwdriver and pliers in place, tinted ochre; the bottom row has the same tools tinted teal, but the pliers' outline is an empty dashed silhouette while the teal pliers hang crooked at the far right, outside any outline."}

There is a test we can run. An integrated density is, by construction, an area multiplied by a mean intensity (up to a constant that depends on the software's units). If that holds for GFAP, and `Pdgfbr_MeanInt` behaves the same way against `Pdgfrb_IntDen` and `Pdgfrb_Area`, then it is playing the role of the PDGFR-β mean intensity.






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Integrated density divided by (area x mean intensity), per animal;
# for a true mean-intensity column this ratio is a constant
ratio_gfap <- cell_data$Gfap_IntDen / (cell_data$Gfap_Area * cell_data$Gfap_MeanInt)
ratio_pdgfrb <- cell_data$Pdgfrb_IntDen / (cell_data$Pdgfrb_Area * cell_data$Pdgfbr_MeanInt)

# Compare the two ratios side by side
rbind(GFAP = summary(ratio_gfap), `PDGFR-β` = summary(ratio_pdgfrb))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
            Min.  1st Qu.   Median      Mean  3rd Qu.     Max.
GFAP    9.892551 9.941829 9.990855  9.998491 10.05885 10.11103
PDGFR-β 9.894142 9.947122 9.998280 10.000256 10.05973 10.10328
```


:::
:::






Both ratios sit at about 10, within a percent or so of each other (the mean intensities are stored as integers, so some rounding noise is expected). The same constant shows up for the two markers, which is what we would see if the column is the PDGFR-β mean intensity and the typo is only in the name. It is evidence, not proof: the constant of 10 is itself something the software settings would explain, and a different quantity could in principle line up by coincidence. So I rename it, I say in the script that I did so on this evidence, and I would still confirm with the person who ran the export.

## Validation rules with `assertr`

Cleaning fixes what we already noticed. Validation is what protects you from what you have *not* noticed yet, and from the next version of the file. The right analogy is the control you run before the samples: you weigh a known 10 g weight before trusting the balance, and you run a no-template control before trusting the PCR. An assertion is a control for the data. It states what must be true, and if it is not, it stops the analysis loudly instead of letting a negative tissue area flow into a model.

Several packages do this. `pointblank` builds polished validation reports, and `validate` keeps the rules as a separate object you can reuse and summarize. I chose `assertr` [@assertr] because it fits the way we already write R in this book: each rule is one step in a `|>` pipeline, it hands the data frame through unchanged when everything passes, and it halts with an error when something fails. That means the rules can sit *inside* the cleaning pipeline, so validated data is the only kind that comes out the other end.

The rules I want for `cell_data` come straight from what we know about the design:

- `MouseID` is unique: one row per animal.
- `Tissue_Area` is positive and in a plausible range.
- `DPI` takes only the days in the protocol: 0, 3, 7, 14, 30.
- SHAM animals exist only at DPI 0. This is the design feature documented in @sec-eda-summary, and encoding it means a future file with a SHAM animal at day 7 would raise an alarm instead of quietly creating an interaction the design cannot support.

The plausible range for the area is the one rule I want to flag as my own judgment. The observed values run from about 6 to 20 mm², and I set the bounds at 5 and 25, a window that leaves a little room on each side of the data (the smallest section is 5.9 mm²) without being a measured limit; the right bounds come from the section size in your acquisition protocol. `within_bounds()` checks values against such a window, `is_uniq` checks the identifier, `in_set()` checks membership, and `verify()` checks any logical statement about the whole table.






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(assertr)

# Five rules in a pipeline; the data pass through unchanged if all hold,
# and the pipeline stops with an error if one fails
cell_data |>
  assert(is_uniq, MouseID) |>
  assert(within_bounds(5, 25), Tissue_Area) |>
  assert(in_set(0, 3, 7, 14, 30), DPI) |>
  verify(Condition != "SHAM" | DPI == 0) |>
  verify(abs(Tissue_Area * 1e6 - Tisue_Area) < 1e-3) |>
  nrow()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] 49
```


:::
:::






All five rules passed, so the data came through and `nrow()` reports its 49 rows. Silence is the success message in `assertr`, which is easy to mistake for nothing having happened.

You may have noticed what is *not* on the list: a rule demanding that no value be missing. `Pdgfrb_Neg` has one `NA` (animal `Td79`), and `assert(not_na, Pdgfrb_Neg)` would stop the pipeline on it. But that `NA` is not a data-entry error to repair; it is a question about how the number came to be missing, and a rule that simply halts on it would hide the question instead of asking it. (A different kind of rule, one checking that a derived column agrees with the columns it is computed from, turns out to matter for exactly this animal; the next chapter shows why.) We take it up properly in @sec-eda-missing-data. The last rule is my favorite, because it guards the decision from the previous section: the day someone updates the file and the two area columns stop agreeing, the script will refuse to drop one of them without telling me.

A validation that has never failed has never been shown to work, just as a positive control that you have never seen light up is not yet a control. So let me break a copy of the data on purpose, with three planted errors: a negative tissue area, a duplicated animal ID, and a SHAM animal at day 7 (@fig-quality-alarm).

![A validation that has never failed has never been shown to work. Test the alarm: plant a deliberate error and listen for it to go off.](images/eda-data-quality_smoke-alarm-test.png){#fig-quality-alarm fig-align="center" width="85%" fig-alt="Watercolor sketch of a researcher in a lab coat standing on a small wooden stepstool, holding a lit candle up under a round smoke detector on the ceiling while covering one ear and grinning. Teal and ochre sound arcs and a small orange light show the detector sounding."}






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# A deliberately broken copy: a negative area, a repeated ID, a SHAM animal after day 0
cell_broken <- cell_data
cell_broken$Tissue_Area[3] <- -1
cell_broken$MouseID[5] <- cell_broken$MouseID[4]
cell_broken$Condition[7] <- "SHAM"

# Run one rule on it and print the error message instead of stopping the render
tryCatch(
  assert(cell_broken, within_bounds(5, 25), Tissue_Area),
  error = function(e) cat(conditionMessage(e))
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
Column 'Tissue_Area' violates assertion 'within_bounds(5, 25)' 1 time
    verb redux_fn            predicate      column index value
1 assert       NA within_bounds(5, 25) Tissue_Area     3    -1

assertr stopped execution
```


:::
:::






This is what a failure looks like: the error names the column, the rule it violated, how many times, and the row index and value of each offender. With the default behavior the pipeline stops at the first failing rule. When I am auditing a new file I would rather see *every* problem at once, and `assertr` supports that with `chain_start()` and `chain_end()`, which collect all the violations instead of stopping at the first.






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Collect every violation at once instead of stopping at the first
cell_broken |>
  chain_start() |>
  assert(is_uniq, MouseID) |>
  assert(within_bounds(5, 25), Tissue_Area) |>
  verify(Condition != "SHAM" | DPI == 0) |>
  chain_end(error_fun = error_return)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[[1]]
Column 'MouseID' violates assertion 'is_uniq' 2 times
    verb redux_fn predicate  column index value
1 assert       NA   is_uniq MouseID     4  Td18
2 assert       NA   is_uniq MouseID     5  Td18

[[2]]
Column 'Tissue_Area' violates assertion 'within_bounds(5, 25)' 1 time
    verb redux_fn            predicate      column index value
1 assert       NA within_bounds(5, 25) Tissue_Area     3    -1

[[3]]
verification [Condition != "SHAM" | DPI == 0] failed! (1 failure)

    verb redux_fn                      predicate column index value
1 verify       NA Condition != "SHAM" | DPI == 0     NA     7    NA
```


:::
:::






The output is a list with one entry per failed rule: the duplicated `Td18` at rows 4 and 5, the negative area at row 3, and, for the `verify()` rule, the fact that the statement was false. (`verify()` can report that it failed but not which rows, which is the price of its generality; when you need the rows, an `assert()` on a derived column is the better tool.) That list is exactly what I would send back to the person who made the file.

## The cleaned object

With the checks defined, assembling `cell_clean` is a single pipeline: validate the raw data as it arrived, standardize the names, then apply the two typo repairs and drop the redundant unit. I validate before cleaning, using the original names, so the rules describe the file as it exists on disk. The renames come after `clean_names()` and say in code what the previous sections argued.






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Validate the raw data, standardize names, then apply the two repairs
cell_clean <- cell_data |>
  assert(is_uniq, MouseID) |>
  assert(within_bounds(5, 25), Tissue_Area) |>
  assert(in_set(0, 3, 7, 14, 30), DPI) |>
  verify(Condition != "SHAM" | DPI == 0) |>
  verify(abs(Tissue_Area * 1e6 - Tisue_Area) < 1e-3) |>
  clean_names() |>
  # Tisue_Area is the raw FIJI output in um^2; Tissue_Area is the same area in mm^2 (checked above); keep mm^2
  select(-tisue_area) |>
  # Pdgfbr_MeanInt is a transposition of Pdgfrb_MeanInt (IntDen = Area x MeanInt check)
  rename(pdgfrb_mean_int = pdgfbr_mean_int)

# Rows and columns of the cleaned object, and which column was dropped
dim(cell_clean)
```

