Twenty columns, typo intact. That is not a bug in this chapter; it is the convention working. The next chapters read `cell_data` from the CSV with its original names, and `cell_clean` ends here.

In your own project the same pipeline would not live inside a chapter. I keep it in a short `clean_data.R` script (or a function in `functions/`) that reads the raw file, runs the rules, and returns the cleaned table; every analysis starts by calling it. That way the raw file stays untouched, the repairs have one home, and a new export of the same experiment goes through exactly the same checks.

::: {.callout-note title="How to report it"}
**Methods.** The raw data file was never edited. Before analysis it was checked in an R script with `assertr` rules: animal identifiers unique; tissue area between 5 and 25 mm²; day post-ischemia in {0, 3, 7, 14, 30}; sham animals only at day 0; and tissue area in mm² equal to the area in µm² divided by 10^6. All rules passed for the 49 animals. The redundant µm² column was dropped and a misspelled column name was corrected in code.
:::

## Take home message

Fixing data by hand in the spreadsheet is fast once and impossible to repeat. Doing it in a script turns every repair into a documented, rerunnable step, which is the gel-image rule applied to tables. `janitor` gave us the vocabulary (`clean_names()`, `get_dupes()`, `remove_constant()`), a unit check showed that `Tisue_Area` is `Tissue_Area` times 10^6^ and can go, and an integrated-density check gave good (not final) evidence that `Pdgfbr_MeanInt` is the PDGFR-β mean intensity with a mistyped name. The first duplication has a documented origin (FIJI wrote µm², I converted to mm²); for the second, the cause is still a hypothesis that the export macro would settle. Finally, `assertr` rules encode what must be true of the data, and we watched one fail on a deliberately broken copy so we know what a failure looks like. The raw CSV was never touched, and the other chapters keep the original names. 

With names fixed and the data validated, two chapters remain before we model. @sec-eda-missing-data takes up the single `NA` in `Pdgfrb_Neg` that no cleaning rule could or should repair, and @sec-eda-ggplot finally draws the data, now that we trust what we are drawing.
